// Any approach to optional where it is a value has problems with structs because their size depends
// on the capability and because they behave differently. Also how does copying work?
// But `Optional[iso T]` or `Optional[mut TOptional[` doesn't work as the parameter since constructing one would always require
// that capability. But `Optional[T]` allows too much flexibility.
public closed value Optional[iso T]
{
    case None;
    case Some(value: T);
}

// Does this cause strangeness when used as a generic parameter? For example, `class Example[mut T]`
// would not permit `Example[mut Foo?]` because the parameter is actually `const`. Also, doesn't
// the independent parameter produce strangeness. If you have `Factory[iso Foo?]`, does that mean
// the holder of the factory can adjust the type output? Perhaps that is ok. Perhaps it is a general
// rule that nested independent parameters are not longer independent?
// But how would this work when making aliases? `Array[independent T]` would need to be consistent.
// But if you have an `mut Array[own Struct]` then an alias to is ought to still be `mut Array[own Struct]`
// not `mut Array[mut Struct]`.
public closed const value Optional[independent T]
{
    case None;
    case Some(value: T);
}

// This seems to block flow typing.
public closed const value Optional[T]
{
    case None;
    case Some(value: T);
}


// Hypothetical syntax for a parameter that has the same capability as self
public closed drop[T] value Optional[T out]
{

}

public closed object None inherits Optional[never]

public drop[T] value Some[self T out] inherits Optional[T]
{
    let value: T;

    // TODO this is still strange because the type of the value parameter ought to be `mut` but it
    // needs to be `iso` or `own` in some cases. Maybe this is ok because the capability of self is
    // determined by the type parameter not the `mut self`?
    init(mut self, .value) { }

    // TODO what is strange here is that if `self` is `iso` how does that work since the initializer
    // only implicitly returns `self`
    init(any self, .value) { }

    // What if instead the correct way is that the self param is always `init` but the return type
    // can be specified with a `const` capability to indicate that it must freeze upon the call to
    // the base initializer. What is strange there is that you still might have a mutable field that
    // you are assigning a `const` reference into.

    // OR

    // The self param can have any capability. There is no `init` capability just an `init` phase
    // before the call to the base initializer. The return type of an initializer always match the
    // `self` parameter. However, implicit recovery is allowed after which is how `mut self` can
    // result in `iso`.

    // TODO the return type must have a capability matching `T`
    public implicit operator as(value: T) -> self Optional[T]
        => Some[T](value);
}

public ref[p] drop[T] value Some[p T out] inherits Optional[T]
{
    let value: T;

    // TODO this is still strange because the type of the value parameter ought to be `mut` but it
    // needs to be `iso` or `own` in some cases.
    init(mut self, .value) { }

    // TODO what is strange here is that if `self` is `iso` how does that work since the initializer
    // only implicitly returns `self`
    init(any self, .value) { }

    // TODO the return type must have a capability matching `T`
    public implicit operator as(value: T) -> p Optional[T]
        => Some[T](value);
}

// -----------------

// TODO this doesn't work because the independent parameter won't vary correctly for structs, it
// will act as if the independent parameter is in a reference type.
public transparent closed drop[T] value Optional[independent T out]
{

}

public closed object None inherits Optional[never]

public transparent drop[T] value Some[independent T out] inherits Optional[T]
{
    let value: T;

    init(mut self, .value) { }
}


// -----------------

public closed drop[T] value Optional[none T out]
{

}

public closed object None inherits Optional[never]

public drop[T] value Some[none T out] inherits Optional[T]
{
    let value: self T;

    init(any self, .value) { }
}

// -----------------

public closed drop[T] value Optional
{
    public out type T;
}

public closed object None inherits Optional
{
    public out type T = never;
}

public drop[T] value Some[none T out] inherits Optional[T]
{
    let value: self T;

    init(any self, .value) { }
}


// -----------------

public closed drop[T] value Optional[self T out]
    where T: not own struct
{
}

public closed object None inherits Optional[never];

public drop[T] value Some[self T out] inherits Optional[T]
    where T: not own struct
{
    let value: T;

    init(any self, .value) { }
}
